1. Optimized Linux File System Layout for a 50 GB Server

A Linux server with 50 GB storage should be partitioned properly
so that rapidly growing log files do not consume all available disk space.
Separating important directories prevents one type of data from affecting the entire system.
A suitable layout is to allocate approximately 1 GB for /boot, 8 GB for /, 7 GB for /var, 10 GB for /var/log, 8 GB for /home, 6 GB for /opt, 4 GB for swap, and keep 6 GB as free/reserved space.
A separate /var/log partition is important because it prevents log files from consuming the space required by the operating system. The maximum size of the log filesystem is limited to 10 GB.
Linux provides the logrotate utility for automatically managing logs. For example:
/var/log/application.
log {
    daily
    rotate 7
    size 100M
    compress
    missingok
    notifempty
}
Partition	Size	Purpose
/boot	        1 GB	 Kernel and boot files
/      	      8 GB	 Operating system
/var	        7 GB	 Variable system data
/var/log	    10 GB	 System/application logs
/home   	    8 GB	 User files
/opt	        6 GB	 Application data
Swap	        4 GB	 Virtual memory
Free space  	6 GB	 Future expansion/emergency
Total	50 GB	
This configuration rotates logs regularly, compresses older logs, and prevents individual log files from growing indefinitely.
Disk usage can be monitored using:
df -h
du -sh /var/log/*
If the log partition approaches 80–90% usage, the administrator can generate an alert and remove or compress older logs.
LVM (Logical Volume Management) can also be used because it provides flexibility to increase the size of logical volumes when additional storage becomes available.
Thus, separating /var/log from the root filesystem, using logrotate, compression, monitoring, and LVM ensures efficient storage utilization. It also prevents logs from exceeding 10 GB and protects critical services from disk-space exhaustion.


Q2. Kernel-Level Optimization for High CPU Workloads

Frequent context switching occurs when the CPU repeatedly changes from one process or thread to another.
Context switching is necessary in a multitasking operating system, but excessive switching creates overhead and reduces CPU performance.
Since hardware upgrades are not possible, the Linux scheduler and kernel-level features should be optimized.
First, the administrator should identify the source of excessive context switching using:
pidstat -w 1
vmstat 1
The top command can also be used to identify processes consuming large amounts of CPU.
